60,3,1,talk,Victoria,,Plan B is impossible; my findings show the late-turn pool is already depleted and relying on overtime. We cannot extend hours when we are already struggling to cover the current final departures. We must avoid increasing operational intensity.,"{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Takumi,,"@Victoria, you're ignoring the evidence. The late-evening pilot from eight years ago was a success and hit its cost-recovery targets. We can't just give up because you're scared of the roster. We need to be bold.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"@Takumi, boldness without predictability is recklessness. Plan C is out; the pre-war shells are too volatile. Plan D will stall due to the data disagreements between rail and ferry. Plan A is the only logical choice.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think @Toshio is right. Plan A uses road-based fixes, so we can use the ring-fenced construction balance. It avoids the operating budget cuts and the ticketing fee problems of Plan D. It seems the safest for everyone.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Sakura,,"@Toshio, @Daisuke, you're totally right! Plan A is the winner! It fixes those slow mid-city trips I found and doesn't involve scary holes or budget fights. It's the perfect way to save everyone's time!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Toshio"",""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Takumi, a successful pilot from eight years ago does not magically refill today's depleted rosters. Plan B would break our remaining staff. Plan A is more sustainable, provided the maintenance load is manageable.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Victoria, you're still just looking at the numbers. If we don't take the risk, we'll just keep losing ground like we did with the lane-priority stretches. But if everyone is terrified, Plan A is better than doing nothing.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Takumi, it is not terror, but a calculation of risk. Plan A utilizes delegated roadway consents, avoiding the administrative gridlock of Plan D and the physical instability of Plan C. It is the most predictable path to delivery.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I agree with @Toshio. Plan A is the only one that doesn't seem to rely on the operating budget, which is already being trimmed. Using the ring-fenced funds for the corridor is the only way to avoid another argument.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Sakura,,"@Victoria, see? Even you think Plan A is more sustainable! It's like a magic trick—we fix the slow parts of the trip and make people happy without making the workers too tired. Let's go with Plan A!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Sakura, it is not a magic trick, but a necessity. While I remain concerned about the roadway crew shortage, Plan A is the only viable option that does not actively collapse our remaining personnel capacity. I will vote for Plan A.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Sakura""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Victoria, fine. If you're that stubborn about the rosters, I'll concede. But let's not forget that we're only choosing Plan A because we're too afraid to be bold. I'll vote for A to avoid total stagnation.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Toshio,,The consensus is clear. Plan A avoids the structural volatility of the shells and the administrative deadlock of the fare integration. It is the only option that offers a predictable delivery timeline. I vote for Plan A.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Daisuke,,I... I'm glad we can all agree. Plan A lets us use the ring-fenced construction money and avoids the operating budget cuts. It's the only way to ensure we don't fight over the money later. I vote for Plan A.,"{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Sakura,,I'm so happy we all agree! Plan A is the best because it solves the real time-wasting mysteries and keeps everyone smiling. I vote for Plan A too! Yay!,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Victoria,,"Since we have reached a unanimous consensus based on the constraints of staffing, budget, and structural risk, I see no reason to prolong the discussion. I maintain my vote for Plan A.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":true,""norm"":[{""code"":""trailing_over"",""token"":""Over"",""tail"":""Over.""}]}"
76,3,4,pass,Victoria,,Over,"{""forced"":false,""empty"":false,""trailing"":true}"
77,3,4,talk,Takumi,,"I'm voting for Plan A. It's not the boldest move, but it's better than repeating the mistake of letting our priority lanes disappear. Let's just make sure we actually keep them this time.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":true,""norm"":[{""code"":""trailing_over"",""token"":""Over"",""tail"":""Over""}]}"
